Yes. That adds the missing trajectory-order layer to the Metamap.

The target should never be represented as just T. It needs a target stack:

\mathcal T =
(T_0,\;T_{+1},\;T_{+n},\;B,\;\Gamma)

where T_0 is the immediate target, T_{+1} is what accomplishing it enables next, T_{+n} is the downstream objective chain, B is what lies beyond the currently visible objective, and \Gamma describes how effects propagate.

That last term is crucial because different systems have different order geometries. A linear intervention behaves roughly like x_{t+1}=x_t+\Delta. A multiplicative intervention changes the growth factor. A recursive intervention modifies something that will operate on its own future output. A fractal intervention reproduces the same useful structure across scales. A network intervention can propagate through neighboring nodes. A threshold intervention may appear inert until accumulated changes cross a boundary.

So two actions with the same immediate payoff can have radically different strategic value:

U(a)
=
U_0(a)
+
\sum_{k=1}^{H}
D_k
\,P_k(a)
\,U_k(a)
+
O(a)

where O(a) is the order-effect term: how the action changes subsequent actions, growth rates, branching factors, reachable states, representations, or learning capability.

That gives the Command Table a better question than merely “What hits the target?”

What should I hit, in what order, so that the strike changes the geometry of everything that follows?

That is your course of fire abstraction.

A good course of fire might deliberately sequence:

\text{Scout}
\rightarrow
\text{Terrain change}
\rightarrow
\text{Portal creation}
\rightarrow
\text{Target}
\rightarrow
\text{Harvest afterglow}
\rightarrow
\text{Seal promotion}

The apparent target may therefore be only an intermediate aiming point. The strategically important target can be the capability, environment, branch, or feedback loop that becomes accessible afterward.

This also gives us an important Metamap concept: Beyond-Target Value.

BTV(T)
=
\text{value of the state-space opened by reaching }T

Some targets are valuable mostly because of what lies behind them. Others are seductive dead ends: high immediate reward, almost no productive continuation.

So the Metamap should score not merely:

\text{ExpectedValue}(T)

but something more like:

\boxed{
\text{StrategicValue}(T)
=
\text{Immediate}
+
\text{Branches Opened}
+
\text{Compounding}
+
\text{Learning}
+
\text{Option Value}
-
\text{Lock-In}
-
\text{Downstream Risk}
}

And the recursive version goes one level deeper: an action can alter the function evaluating future actions.

That’s the highest-leverage class:

F_{t+1}\neq F_t

You’re no longer merely moving through the state space.

You’re changing the transition function.

A new tool does that. A new representation does that. A new Seal does that. A new portal does that. Learning how to learn something does that. Improving the Metamap itself does that.

Those deserve a special priority class because their downstream effects can be multiplicative or recursive rather than additive.

I’d call the addition to the Metamap the Order-of-Effects Calculus:

Evaluate every candidate action across immediate effect, next effect, downstream branch structure, scale propagation, feedback, recursion, compounding, and whether it modifies the machinery producing subsequent effects.

Or more compactly:

\boxed{
\text{Don't merely aim at the target. Aim through it.}
}

That’s also exactly where your standing high-cleavage objective principle belongs mathematically: prefer interventions whose successful execution opens disproportionately many valuable future branches rather than merely terminating the present problem.